
BankAccountSimulator
====================

Simple command-line bank account simulator written in C++.

Overview
--------
This small project demonstrates a minimal Bank Account simulator with a single `Account` class and a CLI-driven `main` program. It's intended as a learning/example project for basic class design, file separation, and simple I/O in C++.

Features
--------
- Create accounts with a starting balance
- Deposit and withdraw funds (basic checks)
- Simple console-based interaction

Prerequisites
-------------
- A C++ compiler supporting C++17 (g++, clang++)
- Unix-like shell (macOS, Linux). The commands below assume macOS (zsh/bash).

Build
-----
Create a `build/` directory and compile the sources. From the project root run:

```bash
mkdir -p build
g++ -std=c++17 -I src -O2 -o build/banksim src/*.cpp
```

This will produce an executable `build/banksim` (the project already contains a `build/` folder used for artifacts).

Run
---
Execute the simulator from the project root:

```bash
./build/banksim
```

The program will run in the terminal and prompt for actions (see source `main.cpp` for details on available commands and flow).

Example
-------
Typical usage looks like:

```text
Welcome to BankAccountSimulator
1) Create account
2) Deposit
3) Withdraw
4) Show balance
5) Exit
> 1
Enter initial balance: 1000
Account created.
> 2
Enter deposit amount: 250
Deposit successful. New balance: 1250
```

Project layout
--------------
- `src/Account.h` - Declaration of the `Account` class and public API.
- `src/Account.cpp` - `Account` class implementation.
- `src/main.cpp` - Entry point and CLI interaction logic.
- `build/` - Output folder for compiled binaries (not committed in git normally).

